1
00:00:00,108 --> 00:00:03,350
These tests were done with
Qwen 3.8 Flash Next,

2
00:00:03,370 --> 00:00:06,831
which runs on a 16 GB card,
at almost 70 tokens per second.

3
00:00:06,851 --> 00:00:08,400
And all of that from a single prompt.

4
00:00:08,420 --> 00:00:09,955
Thing is, it's only half a model.

5
00:00:09,975 --> 00:00:11,830
So, does it replace Claude?

6
00:00:11,850 --> 00:00:13,877
I'm going to show you in this video
what it managed to do,

7
00:00:13,897 --> 00:00:16,383
why it's so strong and how fast
it runs,

8
00:00:16,403 --> 00:00:17,694
and how to install it at home.

9
00:00:17,714 --> 00:00:20,316
And at the end, the mistakes that cost me
quite a bit of time.

10
00:00:20,336 --> 00:00:21,629
First, the results.

11
00:00:21,649 --> 00:00:24,886
On the left, that's Pi, the tool that lets
the AI write files all by itself.

12
00:00:24,906 --> 00:00:27,260
On the right, that's Strata's counter,

13
00:00:27,302 --> 00:00:30,337
the program that runs the model,
in tokens per second.

14
00:00:30,357 --> 00:00:32,850
A token is roughly
three quarters of a word.

15
00:00:32,870 --> 00:00:36,268
I write my prompt in English because
it's much more effective,

16
00:00:36,288 --> 00:00:39,670
since its docs say it's weaker
in other languages,

17
00:00:39,690 --> 00:00:41,149
and I never touch the code.

18
00:00:41,169 --> 00:00:44,558
If it gets stuck, I nudge it with a simple
phrase or word: "continue,"

19
00:00:44,578 --> 00:00:48,100
and I count every nudge like one more
coin at the arcade.

20
00:00:48,137 --> 00:00:50,041
The first test is a 3D island.

21
00:00:50,061 --> 00:00:51,936
It rotates with the mouse, it zooms,

22
00:00:51,956 --> 00:00:55,249
and when I press the N key,
night falls, with stars.

23
00:00:55,269 --> 00:00:58,406
It wrote it in 15 and a half minutes,
with no retries at all.

24
00:00:58,426 --> 00:00:59,482
Next, the brick breaker.

25
00:00:59,502 --> 00:01:01,210
It has lives, a game over screen,

26
00:01:01,230 --> 00:01:05,473
sound effects, and power-ups that widen
the paddle or triple the ball.

27
00:01:05,493 --> 00:01:08,244
That one it did in 8 minutes,
also with no retries.

28
00:01:08,264 --> 00:01:11,372
Then I made the site for a travel agency
that doesn't exist,

29
00:01:11,392 --> 00:01:14,123
with my own photos, Kilimanjaro
big on the home page,

30
00:01:14,143 --> 00:01:17,153
destinations with their prices,
and traveler testimonials.

31
00:01:17,173 --> 00:01:18,972
It really looks like a real agency site.

32
00:01:18,992 --> 00:01:23,513
It took 17 minutes, but following a plan
that I'd written beforehand with Claude,

33
00:01:23,533 --> 00:01:25,526
and I'll explain why I did that
a bit later.

34
00:01:25,546 --> 00:01:26,928
And the hardest one: a mini-Minecraft.

35
00:01:26,948 --> 00:01:29,506
You walk around a world made of cubes,
with trees and terrain,

36
00:01:29,526 --> 00:01:32,787
you jump, you break blocks, you can even
add blocks of dirt, build,

37
00:01:32,807 --> 00:01:34,742
add stone, sand, etc.

38
00:01:34,817 --> 00:01:37,847
Even the pixel textures are
generated in code,

39
00:01:37,913 --> 00:01:39,499
without it ever seeing what it draws.

40
00:01:39,519 --> 00:01:42,603
That one, though, took 3 retries
and just 35 minutes.

41
00:01:42,623 --> 00:01:45,772
To understand these results,
you need to know where this model comes from.

42
00:01:45,792 --> 00:01:48,611
Qwen 3.8 Flash Next, a preview of Qwen 4,

43
00:01:48,660 --> 00:01:51,494
released in August to show off
its new architecture.

44
00:01:51,514 --> 00:01:55,615
And in coding, according to Qwen's tests,
it beats Qwen 3.8 27B,

45
00:01:55,652 --> 00:01:58,242
the model I told you about last Thursday.

46
00:01:58,346 --> 00:02:03,346
On a multi-language coding test,
it scores 81 versus 74 for the 27B.

47
00:02:03,428 --> 00:02:05,327
Except at my place, it doesn't run in full.

48
00:02:05,347 --> 00:02:08,147
With 32 GB of RAM, I can only
take the Coder,

49
00:02:08,167 --> 00:02:11,826
which is the same model cut in half
and kept for code.

50
00:02:11,904 --> 00:02:14,156
Its authors themselves say
it's experimental

51
00:02:14,176 --> 00:02:16,525
and weaker outside of code
and English.

52
00:02:16,545 --> 00:02:18,479
But on the main coding test,

53
00:02:18,499 --> 00:02:21,799
they still keep 95% of the full
model's score.

54
00:02:21,925 --> 00:02:23,111
That's their number.

55
00:02:23,131 --> 00:02:24,935
What it actually does, you just saw.

56
00:02:24,955 --> 00:02:27,689
And even cut in half, it's still
way bigger than my card.

57
00:02:27,709 --> 00:02:31,517
Last Monday, I told you 16 GB is enough
if you pick the right file.

58
00:02:31,537 --> 00:02:32,831
So, how does it fit?

59
00:02:32,851 --> 00:02:36,148
Well, the model is split into
hundreds of little specialists

60
00:02:36,168 --> 00:02:37,593
called experts.

61
00:02:37,613 --> 00:02:40,119
And for each word, it only wakes up
a few of them.

62
00:02:40,139 --> 00:02:44,084
Out of 125 billion parameters,
only 6 billion are at work.

63
00:02:44,104 --> 00:02:46,957
That's what lets it be both huge
and fast.

64
00:02:46,977 --> 00:02:49,374
And Strata is the program
that sorts all of that out.

65
00:02:49,394 --> 00:02:53,210
The card keeps the most-used experts,
the RAM keeps the rest,

66
00:02:53,230 --> 00:02:55,461
and a big table stays on the SSD.

67
00:02:55,611 --> 00:02:58,697
On top of that, Strata is free
and open source.

68
00:02:59,058 --> 00:03:02,451
Now, about speed. To measure it
without the tools in the way,

69
00:03:02,471 --> 00:03:06,620
I asked it for a short story about
a dragon who loves video games.

70
00:03:06,640 --> 00:03:11,105
Almost no thinking, a short answer,
and the monitor shows 51 tokens per second.

71
00:03:11,125 --> 00:03:14,619
During the real tests, with all the code,
it's even faster, weirdly.

72
00:03:14,639 --> 00:03:18,621
Close to 70 on average, and
depending on the test, 43 to 84.

73
00:03:18,773 --> 00:03:22,220
For a reference point, a user with
a 5070 Ti,

74
00:03:22,240 --> 00:03:25,313
with the same processor as me
and about the same RAM,

75
00:03:25,333 --> 00:03:28,535
measures about 70 tokens per second
with this model.

76
00:03:28,655 --> 00:03:32,110
He turned thinking off,
and he doesn't check that the code works.

77
00:03:32,130 --> 00:03:33,398
Mine is in the same range,

78
00:03:33,418 --> 00:03:36,216
but with thinking on,
and code that has to work.

79
00:03:36,236 --> 00:03:40,875
And if your card is more affordable, like
a 16 GB Radeon, with 30 GB of RAM,

80
00:03:40,895 --> 00:03:45,089
another user measures about 37 tokens
per second, still with this model.

81
00:03:45,109 --> 00:03:47,359
Me, I have a 5080, a high-end card,

82
00:03:47,379 --> 00:03:51,539
and its memory is 2 times faster
than a 5060 Ti's, for example.

83
00:03:51,623 --> 00:03:53,817
So my numbers are more the high end
of the range.

84
00:03:53,837 --> 00:03:56,718
On your 16 GB card, it runs too,
just slower.

85
00:03:56,738 --> 00:03:59,145
And speed drops as the
conversation grows.

86
00:03:59,165 --> 00:04:01,545
The project itself publishes that.

87
00:04:01,628 --> 00:04:06,869
On another machine, with an older engine,
the Coder drops from 59 to 43 tokens/s.

88
00:04:06,962 --> 00:04:09,841
So basically, the more code the agent
reads, the slower it gets.

89
00:04:09,861 --> 00:04:10,958
That's why I picked Pi:

90
00:04:10,978 --> 00:04:15,270
its instructions are 1500 tokens,
versus 7400 for OpenCode.

91
00:04:15,326 --> 00:04:17,390
That's less for it to reread every time.

92
00:04:17,410 --> 00:04:20,820
To install it, Strata has a simple rule:
your RAM must hold the experts,

93
00:04:20,840 --> 00:04:22,746
plus about ten GB for Windows.

94
00:04:22,766 --> 00:04:26,109
That's why with 32 GB,
its docs only offer me the Coder.

95
00:04:26,129 --> 00:04:28,389
Then there are about sixty GB
to download.

96
00:04:28,409 --> 00:04:31,198
At my place, between clicking Download
and my first message,

97
00:04:31,218 --> 00:04:32,986
a bit less than half an hour went by.

98
00:04:33,006 --> 00:04:36,515
Before launching it, you have to close
everything, browser included,

99
00:04:36,535 --> 00:04:41,092
because while it writes,
my RAM is full at 30.8 GB out of 31.

100
00:04:41,155 --> 00:04:44,190
And look at this box on the monitor,
it's the disk reading.

101
00:04:44,210 --> 00:04:45,535
At rest, it's at zero.

102
00:04:45,555 --> 00:04:48,310
As soon as it writes, it goes up to
around 200 MB per second.

103
00:04:48,330 --> 00:04:51,218
The RAM is full, so part of it
goes through the SSD.

104
00:04:51,238 --> 00:04:52,322
Next, I plug in Pi.

105
00:04:52,342 --> 00:04:55,846
Strata's app gives me an address
and I put it in Pi's file

106
00:04:55,866 --> 00:04:57,126
with the model's name.

107
00:04:57,146 --> 00:04:59,429
As for the thinking, though,
it's a bit trickier.

108
00:04:59,449 --> 00:05:01,529
This model thinks for a long time
before it writes,

109
00:05:01,549 --> 00:05:04,794
and that thinking counts toward the maximum
length of its answer.

110
00:05:04,814 --> 00:05:06,694
By default, Pi gives it 16,000 tokens,

111
00:05:06,714 --> 00:05:09,188
and that's also what Strata would
write for it on my machine.

112
00:05:09,208 --> 00:05:12,983
A full game plus the thinking can
overflow, and the file stops halfway.

113
00:05:13,003 --> 00:05:17,067
So before the first test, I raised
that limit by hand, to 32,000 tokens.

114
00:05:17,087 --> 00:05:19,000
All the files are in the description.

115
00:05:19,020 --> 00:05:20,838
And right there is my first mistake.

116
00:05:20,858 --> 00:05:22,862
32,000 tokens wasn't enough.

117
00:05:22,882 --> 00:05:25,598
Once, it got cut off dead
in the middle of the site,

118
00:05:25,618 --> 00:05:29,621
7 minutes lost, and then
it had to write the file in chunks.

119
00:05:29,768 --> 00:05:32,080
So if you redo this,
go even higher.

120
00:05:32,100 --> 00:05:33,995
The site, though, wasn't
done on the first try.

121
00:05:34,015 --> 00:05:37,035
On the first try, I ask it for
a home screen that slowly zooms,

122
00:05:37,055 --> 00:05:39,357
6 destinations, reviews that
scroll by themselves,

123
00:05:39,377 --> 00:05:42,774
a full-screen gallery, and the whole
thing has to fit on a phone.

124
00:05:42,794 --> 00:05:45,056
It writes everything in half an hour,
but in English,

125
00:05:45,076 --> 00:05:46,993
even though I asked for French.

126
00:05:47,013 --> 00:05:48,663
And at the end, it wants to test itself.

127
00:05:48,683 --> 00:05:52,393
It opens an invisible Chrome browser,
and Chrome never hands control back.

128
00:05:52,413 --> 00:05:55,598
So it stayed stuck for almost 2 hours
until I stopped it.

129
00:05:55,618 --> 00:05:58,940
For the second try, I gave
the photos to Claude and asked it

130
00:05:58,960 --> 00:06:00,836
to draw me up a plan for this model,

131
00:06:00,856 --> 00:06:03,763
with the steps and all the text
already written in French.

132
00:06:03,783 --> 00:06:07,015
The local model only had to follow
the plan, step by step.

133
00:06:07,035 --> 00:06:10,242
That gave the site you saw,
in 17 minutes, with zero errors,

134
00:06:10,262 --> 00:06:13,193
even though it got stuck 2 more times
because of Chrome.

135
00:06:13,213 --> 00:06:16,438
You do need to know that a few lines
of the page were already in the plan.

136
00:06:16,458 --> 00:06:19,542
And the plan changed the order.
Well, there, Claude got it wrong.

137
00:06:19,562 --> 00:06:22,549
There was no more full-screen gallery,
and the reviews no longer scrolled.

138
00:06:22,569 --> 00:06:24,750
Instead, there's a slideshow
on the home page

139
00:06:24,770 --> 00:06:27,264
and a card that opens
for each destination.

140
00:06:27,284 --> 00:06:29,162
For the mini-Minecraft, the mistake
was its own.

141
00:06:29,182 --> 00:06:32,365
On the first try, it tells me:
"file written and checked."

142
00:06:32,385 --> 00:06:34,799
Except the folder was empty,
it hadn't written anything at all.

143
00:06:34,819 --> 00:06:35,924
It took 3 retries,

144
00:06:35,944 --> 00:06:39,364
and on the last one, it even found
a bug in its own code.

145
00:06:39,384 --> 00:06:42,611
So, in total, with all those freezes
on Chrome, I spent nearly 2 hours

146
00:06:42,631 --> 00:06:44,962
waiting for nothing. But actually,
it wasn't because of the code,

147
00:06:44,982 --> 00:06:46,799
it was more because of the tool:

148
00:06:46,819 --> 00:06:49,508
Pi was waiting for an answer
that Chrome never gave.

149
00:06:49,528 --> 00:06:52,510
So tell it not to open a browser
to test itself,

150
00:06:52,530 --> 00:06:55,188
and if it stays stuck more than 3 minutes
on a command,

151
00:06:55,208 --> 00:06:56,683
just press Esc.

152
00:06:56,703 --> 00:06:59,213
But what interested me most
about the model, actually,

153
00:06:59,233 --> 00:07:03,195
is that it writes its own test scripts
without anyone asking it to.

154
00:07:03,215 --> 00:07:04,135
So, what about Claude?

155
00:07:04,155 --> 00:07:07,553
After the fiasco it caused
by changing my instructions,

156
00:07:07,573 --> 00:07:09,741
it was still super efficient.

157
00:07:09,806 --> 00:07:11,660
I didn't give it my prompts
word for word,

158
00:07:11,680 --> 00:07:13,728
so I'm not going to stage a fake showdown.

159
00:07:13,748 --> 00:07:17,003
I just asked it for a travel site
with my photos, in one sentence.

160
00:07:17,023 --> 00:07:20,053
It did it in under 2 and a half minutes,
in French, and it holds up.

161
00:07:20,073 --> 00:07:22,033
My model, meanwhile, took half an hour,

162
00:07:22,053 --> 00:07:24,668
and in English, with a much
more detailed request.

163
00:07:24,688 --> 00:07:26,961
On my machine, the engine never crashed
during my tests.

164
00:07:26,981 --> 00:07:28,889
For others, that's not always the case.

165
00:07:28,909 --> 00:07:31,947
On GitHub, a still-open ticket
describes the engine crashing

166
00:07:31,967 --> 00:07:34,770
with this Coder on certain
long requests.

167
00:07:34,790 --> 00:07:37,611
Several users hit it
on an 8 to 12 GB card,

168
00:07:37,631 --> 00:07:39,793
and the error message blames the RAM.

169
00:07:39,813 --> 00:07:42,658
Except actually, users found
the real cause:

170
00:07:42,678 --> 00:07:44,674
it's an engine bug.
A fix has been proposed,

171
00:07:44,694 --> 00:07:47,059
but it's not in the version
I tested yet.

172
00:07:47,079 --> 00:07:51,522
A 5080 user also reports
another error: "non-finite logits,"

173
00:07:51,542 --> 00:07:53,453
with other files of the same model.

174
00:07:53,473 --> 00:07:57,447
And another tester saw this Coder
fail a coding task in Claude Code,

175
00:07:57,467 --> 00:07:59,874
where other versions of the model
got it right.

176
00:07:59,894 --> 00:08:01,529
Mind you, Strata is very young:

177
00:08:01,549 --> 00:08:06,370
the project was created on September 24,
and it ships a version every 2 to 3 days.

178
00:08:06,436 --> 00:08:07,588
So note your version carefully.

179
00:08:07,608 --> 00:08:09,934
And if it breaks on your end,
copy the exact message,

180
00:08:09,954 --> 00:08:13,356
because that's how the cause
of this bug was found.

181
00:08:13,376 --> 00:08:16,656
So, does it replace my subscription
or maybe even yours?

182
00:08:16,676 --> 00:08:17,969
I don't think it fully does.

183
00:08:17,989 --> 00:08:20,384
That said, with the 20-dollar-a-month
subscription,

184
00:08:20,404 --> 00:08:24,079
Claude can write the plan and the local model
runs it on your card.

185
00:08:24,099 --> 00:08:27,744
That's exactly what I do,
and that's exactly what saved the site.

186
00:08:27,764 --> 00:08:30,066
And even without a plan, it does very well:

187
00:08:30,086 --> 00:08:32,550
the island, the brick breaker, all done
quickly and with no retries,

188
00:08:32,570 --> 00:08:34,051
and even the mini-Minecraft.

189
00:08:34,071 --> 00:08:37,179
So, if this video saved you from
a bad purchase, subscribe.

190
00:08:37,199 --> 00:08:39,606
And if you want to redo it tonight at home,
here's the list:

191
00:08:39,626 --> 00:08:42,560
1, you need a card with 12 to 16 GB,

192
00:08:42,580 --> 00:08:45,368
maybe we'll try to do it with
an 8 GB card,

193
00:08:45,388 --> 00:08:48,903
at least 32 GB of RAM, and about 90 GB
free on the disk.

194
00:08:48,923 --> 00:08:51,901
2, you install the Strata app,
the link is in the description.

195
00:08:51,921 --> 00:08:54,791
In its model list, it tells you
whether each one fits on your PC

196
00:08:54,811 --> 00:08:56,419
before you even download it.

197
00:08:56,439 --> 00:08:58,852
3, if you take the Coder, without the images,

198
00:08:58,872 --> 00:09:02,018
and before launching, you close everything,
browser included.

199
00:09:02,038 --> 00:09:04,790
4, you plug in Pi with the address
the app gives you,

200
00:09:04,810 --> 00:09:08,744
and you raise your response limits
higher than my 32,000 tokens.

201
00:09:08,842 --> 00:09:10,082
The file is in the description.

202
00:09:10,102 --> 00:09:12,387
And if you have more RAM,
according to the docs,

203
00:09:12,407 --> 00:09:15,367
at 48 GB, you move up to the small sizes
of the full model,

204
00:09:15,387 --> 00:09:17,126
and at 64, you can take them all.

205
00:09:17,146 --> 00:09:21,193
And if this is your very first local AI,
start with the video on LM Studio instead,

206
00:09:21,213 --> 00:09:22,436
I'll put it right there.

207
00:09:22,456 --> 00:09:25,830
See you next time for another
video. Bye!
